WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.840
I was trying to think today,
what is it about Weezer?

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.083
What is it about your music,
Rivers, that gets to me?

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.680
I will say

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.030
it is incredibly

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.203
unique.

00:00:15.203 --> 00:00:17.400
Like it is, there's no one else.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.620
I just keep listening to the riffs.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.630
I keep listening to the
construction of the song

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.170
and it just never sounds
like anybody else.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.600
And I mean that in the
most complimentary way.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.200
It's just, I always know
it's you when the song starts

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.480
and it's just fantastic.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.920
And I don't know.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.750
So I guess part of where I wanted to start

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.930
was figuring out where that comes from,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.280
your approach to music,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.840
because you have such a unique,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.920
individualistic

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.390
approach to it.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.790
When you were a kid, what
were you listening to?

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.520
Oh, I'm, I'm actually thinking
of The Sweater Song now,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.490
cuz you just mentioned it, but
I remember the moment I came

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.450
up with that riff.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.283
Yeah.

00:00:58.283 --> 00:01:02.414
And I had, I was about 20, 21 years old

00:01:02.414 --> 00:01:06.330
and I had been exposed to cool
music finally after moving

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.853
to LA from Connecticut.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.380
Got a job at Tower Records
and started listening

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.920
to Sonic Youth and Pixies
and Velvet Underground.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.007
And I was like,

00:01:18.007 --> 00:01:21.300
"Okay, I'm gonna try to write
a Velvet Underground song."

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.991
And I sat down and came up
with The Sweater Song riff.

00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:25.020
Mhm.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.117
And I was like, "Yes, this is so cool."

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.920
And, you know, a couple
years went by, we got signed,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.160
put out a record, it blew up, very happy.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.060
1995, we end up playing this big festival

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.960
in New York City and Lars
Ulrich was there from Metallica

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.250
and I saw him backstage.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.083
Yeah.

00:01:45.083 --> 00:01:47.670
And the truth was, I was
a huge metal head in -

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:48.503
Sure.

00:01:48.503 --> 00:01:49.336
In high school.

00:01:49.336 --> 00:01:51.360
And that's really how I learned
how to play my instrument.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.670
It suddenly occurred to
me like The Sweater Song,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.730
that riff, that's actually very similar

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.603
to Metallica's Sanitarium.

00:01:58.603 --> 00:02:00.417
(laughter)

00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:02.400
And if you play them next to each other,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.175
they're pretty much identical.

00:02:04.175 --> 00:02:05.063
(laughter)
Just one-

00:02:06.212 --> 00:02:07.045
(Laughter)

00:02:07.045 --> 00:02:08.273
The Sweater Song's in a major key.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.370
So I had to tell him that
right then and there.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.480
That's what I told him when I met him.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.430
And how did Lars take it?

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.843
He's totally cool.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.480
We just ran into him again.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.050
Yeah, he's just very, very cool.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.816
And cuz recently we
covered Enter Sandman -

00:02:25.816 --> 00:02:26.649
Mhm

00:02:26.649 --> 00:02:29.070
It's been in our set
recently and we ended up

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.870
playing with him this summer

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.470
and we were so close to
playing Enter Sandman

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.300
right before they went on.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.097
And, but, and we asked him like,

00:02:39.097 --> 00:02:40.170
"Hey, is it cool if we play it?"

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.003
And he said,

00:02:41.003 --> 00:02:42.141
"Yeah, that would mean we
don't have to play it."

00:02:42.141 --> 00:02:44.460
(laughter)

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:45.293
So did you play it?

00:02:45.293 --> 00:02:46.126
No, we didn't.

00:02:46.126 --> 00:02:46.959
We chickened out.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:47.792
Oh man.

00:02:47.792 --> 00:02:48.750
That would've been amazing.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:49.583
It would've.

00:02:49.583 --> 00:02:50.453
What would the crowd
have done, I guess, say,

00:02:50.453 --> 00:02:52.170
I mean, it would be -
That's the question.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.460
Yes.
(laughter)

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.930
that is the question.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.610
Well Metallica would have
to play The Sweater Song.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:57.990
I know, yeah, yeah.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.540
Um, I'd pay to see that too.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.180
I, it reminds me of,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.390
I know a comedian who's also a musician

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.970
is really a brilliant
guy named Andy Breckman,

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.440
who's written for Saturday Night Live

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:11.970
and a million other things.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:15.670
And he famously opened for Don McLean

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.720
once at a festival.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.970
And what he did -
Oh no.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.130
He played American Pie
(laughter)

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:27.783
and did the whole thing and
Don McLean was not happy.

00:03:28.788 --> 00:03:31.038
(laughter)

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:33.810
It just kills me.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:34.890
That just kills me.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.870
But yeah, I mean,
Metallica could handle it

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.940
cuz they've got a million songs, you know.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.683
Not so Don McLean, sorry Don.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.130
Well, he's got the Van Gogh song.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.270
He's listening.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.140
Oh, trust me.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.118
Avid fan.

00:03:47.118 --> 00:03:47.951
Avid fan.

